## Output / Outcome

The program successfully implements and compares Ordinary BST and Threaded BST.

1. Both trees produce the same inorder traversal in sorted order.
2. Ordinary BST uses an auxiliary stack and displays its maximum stack usage.
3. Threaded BST performs traversal without recursion or an additional stack.
4. The program displays the predecessor and successor threads of each node, wherever present.
5. Both traversals have O(n) time complexity.
6. Ordinary BST requires O(h) auxiliary space, while Threaded BST requires O(1) auxiliary space for traversal.

### Result
The Threaded BST provides more space-efficient inorder traversal compared to the Ordinary BST by eliminating the need for an auxiliary stack.
